Z||000000||mbramble||09-08-2001||06:57 PM||||Most of you have seen the Mar-Hyde Trim Paint with the picture of the Fiero on it.  Today I saw another product with a Fiero on the can.  This is Mar-Hyde Flexible Bumper Paint Remover.  Where the trim paint shows the back of the car, the paint remover shows the front...  After I just spent all day sanding the paint off my coupe and GT fascias.........<P>Thought I put this in the General Fiero Discussion...  Oh well...<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||09-08-2001||07:12 PM||||Feel better, your much better off sanding than the shortcut. Remover would be ok if it had a whole bunch of previous paint jobs. Just go light on the primer, just use enough to cover it,thinner the coat the better. Less liable to crack down the road.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||09-08-2001||10:26 PM||||I've been wondering if there is a chemical product for removing paint from the plastic body parts. I just picked up a car that has been repainted and they painted over everything, trim and all. It's one of the worst paint jobs I've ever seen. What a PITA it will be to repaint this one. I just feel like dipping the whole car in stripper. I tested some stripper on a junk fender today and it did soften the plastic. I wonder how well this Mar-Hyde stuff works.||||reg||1
